1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,008
What's today's video about? A historical figure.

2
00:00:10,008 --> 00:00:11,508
Scene 1: The Samurai Game and Miyamoto Musashi

3
00:00:11,508 --> 00:00:16,308
I played a new game last night.

4
00:00:16,330 --> 00:00:18,787
What kind of game?

5
00:00:18,811 --> 00:00:22,945
It's a samurai game from Japan.

6
00:00:22,968 --> 00:00:25,901
It was really fun.

7
00:00:25,924 --> 00:00:27,964
That sounds great!

8
00:00:27,988 --> 00:00:30,721
Who appears in it?

9
00:00:30,744 --> 00:00:34,480
A man named Miyamoto Musashi.

10
00:00:34,504 --> 00:00:38,537
Oh, Miyamoto Musashi?

11
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:41,527
He's a very famous figure.

12
00:00:41,550 --> 00:00:43,240
Yes, he is.

13
00:00:43,264 --> 00:00:46,997
But I don’t know much about him.

14
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:49,273
What kind of person was he?

15
00:00:49,297 --> 00:00:55,464
Miyamoto Musashi was a legendary swordsman from old Japan.

16
00:00:55,488 --> 00:00:57,421
A swordsman?

17
00:00:57,444 --> 00:01:00,611
How strong was he?

18
00:01:00,634 --> 00:01:04,003
He fought in over 60 battles.

19
00:01:04,026 --> 00:01:07,714
And he never lost a single one.

20
00:01:07,738 --> 00:01:12,515
Wait, he never lost?

21
00:01:12,538 --> 00:01:15,033
That’s incredible!

22
00:01:15,057 --> 00:01:16,900
Yes, he is.

23
00:01:16,923 --> 00:01:21,057
He’s said to be the strongest in Japan.

24
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:25,972
In the game, he was wielding two swords.

25
00:01:25,995 --> 00:01:27,886
That’s right.

26
00:01:27,910 --> 00:01:32,310
Musashi fought using two swords.

27
00:01:32,333 --> 00:01:34,700
Isn’t one sword the usual?

28
00:01:34,723 --> 00:01:36,656
Yes, he is.

29
00:01:36,679 --> 00:01:41,412
Normally, yes, but Musashi used two.

30
00:01:41,435 --> 00:01:44,371
That’s unusual.

31
00:01:44,395 --> 00:01:47,428
His style looks so cool.

32
00:01:47,451 --> 00:01:49,318
It really does.

33
00:01:49,341 --> 00:01:53,994
That’s why he’s still popular in movies and anime today.

34
00:01:54,018 --> 00:01:58,957
Was Musashi only known for fighting?

35
00:01:58,981 --> 00:02:02,989
No, he was also skilled at painting.

36
00:02:03,012 --> 00:02:05,462
And he wrote books.

37
00:02:05,486 --> 00:02:08,931
Wow, he wrote books too?

38
00:02:08,955 --> 00:02:11,288
What kind of books?

39
00:02:11,311 --> 00:02:14,544
A book called *The Book of Five Rings*.

40
00:02:14,567 --> 00:02:17,251
It’s about strategy and combat.

41
00:02:17,275 --> 00:02:21,512
Can people still read that book today?

42
00:02:21,535 --> 00:02:23,268
Yes, he is.

43
00:02:23,291 --> 00:02:26,170
It’s read all over the world!

44
00:02:26,194 --> 00:02:29,162
He’s famous even outside Japan, huh?

45
00:02:29,185 --> 00:02:31,038
That’s right.

46
00:02:31,061 --> 00:02:33,795
Businesspeople read it a lot too.

47
00:02:33,817 --> 00:02:38,417
Businesspeople read a book about combat?

48
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:40,882
That’s interesting!

49
00:02:40,906 --> 00:02:44,514
It’s because his way of thinking is useful.

50
00:02:44,537 --> 00:02:48,419
Now I want to read that book too.

51
00:02:48,443 --> 00:02:53,276
I’ll lend you a simplified Japanese version next time.

52
00:02:53,299 --> 00:02:55,066
Thanks!

53
00:02:55,089 --> 00:02:58,222
I’ll work harder on the game too.

54
00:02:58,245 --> 00:03:07,254
You can download today’s video script from the description and top comment.

55
00:03:07,254 --> 00:03:08,754
Scene 2: Oda Nobunaga and Azuchi Castle

56
00:03:08,754 --> 00:03:13,165
Where is this castle in the photo from?

57
00:03:13,188 --> 00:03:16,846
That’s a painting of Azuchi Castle.

58
00:03:16,870 --> 00:03:20,241
It was Oda Nobunaga’s castle.

59
00:03:20,265 --> 00:03:23,065
Oda Nobunaga?

60
00:03:23,088 --> 00:03:26,654
I’ve heard the name before.

61
00:03:26,677 --> 00:03:31,415
He’s one of the most famous samurai in Japan.

62
00:03:31,439 --> 00:03:35,640
What kind of person was Nobunaga?

63
00:03:35,664 --> 00:03:41,130
He was incredibly strong and loved new things.

64
00:03:41,153 --> 00:03:44,356
New things?

65
00:03:44,380 --> 00:03:47,277
Like what, for example?

66
00:03:47,300 --> 00:03:51,867
He loved foreign bread and wine.

67
00:03:51,890 --> 00:03:58,423
Wow, a samurai from the past enjoying bread and wine?

68
00:03:58,446 --> 00:04:00,141
Yes, he is.

69
00:04:00,164 --> 00:04:05,931
He even wore foreign clothes and hats.

70
00:04:05,954 --> 00:04:08,621
That’s incredible!

71
00:04:08,644 --> 00:04:11,930
That’s really stylish!

72
00:04:11,954 --> 00:04:13,638
It sure is.

73
00:04:13,661 --> 00:04:17,097
He also owned clocks and globes.

74
00:04:17,121 --> 00:04:20,488
He even had a globe?

75
00:04:20,511 --> 00:04:22,844
That’s amazing!

76
00:04:22,867 --> 00:04:27,565
He was very interested in foreign cultures.

77
00:04:27,589 --> 00:04:30,589
Did he use guns too?

78
00:04:30,612 --> 00:04:32,278
Yes, he is.

79
00:04:32,301 --> 00:04:37,435
Nobunaga used many guns in battle.

80
00:04:37,458 --> 00:04:41,024
That’s how he won his fights, right?

81
00:04:41,047 --> 00:04:42,781
Yes, he is.

82
00:04:42,804 --> 00:04:48,970
He tried to unify Japan by changing old ways of thinking.

83
00:04:48,993 --> 00:04:53,227
He must have been very intelligent.

84
00:04:53,249 --> 00:04:54,939
It really does.

85
00:04:54,962 --> 00:05:01,037
But he was very strict, so he had many enemies.

86
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:03,660
He was strict?

87
00:05:03,683 --> 00:05:05,416
Yes, he is.

88
00:05:05,439 --> 00:05:09,172
In the end, he was betrayed by his own men.

89
00:05:09,195 --> 00:05:13,689
Oh, that’s such a sad part of history.

90
00:05:13,712 --> 00:05:19,952
But Nobunaga’s ideas changed Japan afterward.

91
00:05:19,976 --> 00:05:23,342
Does he appear in anime or dramas often?

92
00:05:23,365 --> 00:05:24,932
Yes, he is.

93
00:05:24,955 --> 00:05:30,355
He’s featured in many works as a cool boss.

94
00:05:30,378 --> 00:05:34,545
That’s why I knew his name!

95
00:05:34,568 --> 00:05:36,101
Yes, he is.

96
00:05:36,124 --> 00:05:41,124
His portrait is always in textbooks.

97
00:05:41,147 --> 00:05:46,914
I’ll have to watch a movie about Nobunaga sometime.

98
00:05:46,937 --> 00:05:49,770
Yes, you should!

99
00:05:49,793 --> 00:05:53,365
It’s really interesting.

100
00:05:53,389 --> 00:06:00,494
The link to review today’s video is in the description and top comment. *Note: The site may be removed without notice.

101
00:06:00,494 --> 00:06:01,994
Scene 3: The Shinsengumi and Okita Soji

102
00:06:01,994 --> 00:06:07,727
Kyoto has so many beautiful old buildings.

103
00:06:07,750 --> 00:06:09,717
It sure is.

104
00:06:09,740 --> 00:06:14,540
This is a place tied to the Shinsengumi.

105
00:06:14,563 --> 00:06:17,896
The Shinsengumi?

106
00:06:17,919 --> 00:06:20,686
I’ve heard of them.

107
00:06:20,709 --> 00:06:26,509
They were a group of samurai who protected Kyoto long ago.

108
00:06:26,532 --> 00:06:29,707
Who was part of the group?

109
00:06:29,731 --> 00:06:35,570
There was a swordsman named Okita Soji, the strongest of them all.

110
00:06:35,593 --> 00:06:38,289
Okita Soji?

111
00:06:38,312 --> 00:06:41,246
What kind of person was he?

112
00:06:41,269 --> 00:06:47,235
He was said to be very young with a cute smile.

113
00:06:47,258 --> 00:06:52,828
Wow, strong but with a cute smile?

114
00:06:52,852 --> 00:06:54,485
Yes, he is.

115
00:06:54,508 --> 00:06:58,241
I loved playing with kids.

116
00:06:58,264 --> 00:07:01,558
He must have been a kind person.

117
00:07:01,582 --> 00:07:06,949
But once he held a sword, he was stronger than anyone.

118
00:07:06,972 --> 00:07:10,582
That contrast is so cool.

119
00:07:10,606 --> 00:07:15,982
They say Okita’s sword speed was incredibly fast.

120
00:07:16,005 --> 00:07:20,072
No one could beat him, could they?

121
00:07:20,094 --> 00:07:25,900
Yes, he was called the greatest genius in the Shinsengumi.

122
00:07:25,924 --> 00:07:30,003
How old was Sōji when he passed away?

123
00:07:30,027 --> 00:07:35,594
Actually, he fell ill and died in his twenties.

124
00:07:35,617 --> 00:07:40,550
Oh no, he died that young?

125
00:07:40,573 --> 00:07:42,179
Yes, he is.

126
00:07:42,202 --> 00:07:45,736
His life was far too short.

127
00:07:45,760 --> 00:07:51,093
That’s why he lives on in everyone’s hearts.

128
00:07:51,116 --> 00:07:53,449
Exactly.

129
00:07:53,472 --> 00:07:57,839
He’s a super popular character in anime and games too.

130
00:07:57,862 --> 00:08:01,238
Which anime does he appear in?

131
00:08:01,261 --> 00:08:07,261
Oh, there are so many—like *Fate* and *Hakuouki*.

132
00:08:07,284 --> 00:08:10,851
Ah, I know those series!

133
00:08:10,874 --> 00:08:14,440
The characters are so cool.

134
00:08:14,463 --> 00:08:19,297
And their inspiration is this very man—Okita Sōji.

135
00:08:19,320 --> 00:08:23,772
I’m so glad I got to come here today.

136
00:08:23,796 --> 00:08:28,840
His temple is nearby too—let’s go visit!

137
00:08:28,863 --> 00:08:30,630
Yes, he is.

138
00:08:30,653 --> 00:08:33,420
Please show me the way!

139
00:08:33,443 --> 00:08:34,943
Scene 4: Tea Culture and Rikyū

140
00:08:34,943 --> 00:08:38,961
This matcha is absolutely delicious.

141
00:08:38,985 --> 00:08:41,161
I’m glad you like it.

142
00:08:41,184 --> 00:08:45,589
This tea room is so peaceful and calming.

143
00:08:45,613 --> 00:08:47,017
Yes, he is.

144
00:08:47,040 --> 00:08:51,973
Who created Japan’s tea culture?

145
00:08:51,996 --> 00:08:55,663
A man named Sen no Rikyū did.

146
00:08:55,686 --> 00:08:58,375
Sen no Rikyū?

147
00:08:58,399 --> 00:09:00,765
When was he alive?

148
00:09:00,788 --> 00:09:07,555
He lived during the Sengoku period, the same era as Oda Nobunaga and Toyotomi Hideyoshi.

149
00:09:07,578 --> 00:09:10,811
So he was from the age of samurai?

150
00:09:10,834 --> 00:09:12,607
Yes, he is.

151
00:09:12,630 --> 00:09:16,964
He taught tea ceremonies to samurai.

152
00:09:16,987 --> 00:09:20,387
Samurai drank tea too?

153
00:09:20,410 --> 00:09:22,068
It really does.

154
00:09:22,091 --> 00:09:27,928
They drank it before battles to calm their minds.

155
00:09:27,952 --> 00:09:29,624
I see.

156
00:09:29,647 --> 00:09:33,585
Tea time was really important, wasn’t it?

157
00:09:33,608 --> 00:09:39,046
Rikyū valued the concept of *wabi-sabi*.

158
00:09:39,070 --> 00:09:41,636
*Wabi-sabi*?

159
00:09:41,659 --> 00:09:43,873
What does that mean?

160
00:09:43,897 --> 00:09:50,497
It’s the beauty of quiet simplicity—elegance without excess.

161
00:09:50,520 --> 00:09:55,053
So that’s why tea rooms are small and simple?

162
00:09:55,076 --> 00:09:57,843
Exactly.

163
00:09:57,866 --> 00:10:03,351
Instead of fancy dishes, he used simple utensils.

164
00:10:03,374 --> 00:10:07,607
The entrance to the room is really small too.

165
00:10:07,630 --> 00:10:10,637
You noticed that!

166
00:10:10,660 --> 00:10:15,860
Samurai even had to leave their swords outside.

167
00:10:15,883 --> 00:10:18,083
Wow.

168
00:10:18,107 --> 00:10:21,740
So inside, everyone was equal?

169
00:10:21,763 --> 00:10:23,297
Yes, he is.

170
00:10:23,320 --> 00:10:29,053
That was the rule—shoguns and samurai, all the same.

171
00:10:29,076 --> 00:10:32,442
What a wonderful idea.

172
00:10:32,466 --> 00:10:37,675
Thanks to Rikyu, the Japanese tea ceremony was perfected.

173
00:10:37,699 --> 00:10:41,783
What kind of person was Rikyu?

174
00:10:41,806 --> 00:10:47,406
He was a man of unshakable principles—never compromising his style.

175
00:10:47,429 --> 00:10:52,198
That’s why he was able to create such a groundbreaking culture, right?

176
00:10:52,221 --> 00:10:53,824
Yes, he is.

177
00:10:53,848 --> 00:10:59,525
Even today, his influence is deeply embedded in Japanese design.

178
00:10:59,548 --> 00:11:04,446
It’s so wonderful to sip tea while learning about history.

179
00:11:04,470 --> 00:11:08,526
Shall I pour you another cup of tea?

180
00:11:08,550 --> 00:11:09,583
Yes, he is.

181
00:11:09,606 --> 00:11:11,373
Yes, please.

182
00:11:11,396 --> 00:11:12,896
Scene 5: The Success Story of Toyotomi Hideyoshi

183
00:11:12,896 --> 00:11:19,163
Wow, Osaka Castle is so grand and magnificent!

184
00:11:19,186 --> 00:11:21,477
It really is!

185
00:11:21,500 --> 00:11:26,583
This castle was built by Toyotomi Hideyoshi.

186
00:11:26,606 --> 00:11:29,543
Toyotomi Hideyoshi?

187
00:11:29,567 --> 00:11:31,968
What kind of person was he?

188
00:11:31,992 --> 00:11:38,449
Hideyoshi’s life is the most dramatic story in Japanese history.

189
00:11:38,472 --> 00:11:40,406
Why do you say that?

190
00:11:40,429 --> 00:11:45,674
He started out dirt-poor, just an ordinary farmer.

191
00:11:45,697 --> 00:11:49,378
He began as a farmer?

192
00:11:49,401 --> 00:11:51,035
Yes, he is.

193
00:11:51,058 --> 00:11:54,191
He had no money, no family—nothing.

194
00:11:54,214 --> 00:11:59,047
How did he rise to become the leader of Japan?

195
00:11:59,070 --> 00:12:04,904
He became a retainer for Oda Nobunaga and worked tirelessly.

196
00:12:04,927 --> 00:12:07,860
What kind of work did he do?

197
00:12:07,883 --> 00:12:13,180
He warmed Nobunaga’s sandals with his own chest before handing them over.

198
00:12:13,204 --> 00:12:17,571
He warmed his shoes for him?

199
00:12:17,594 --> 00:12:19,293
Yes, he is.

200
00:12:19,316 --> 00:12:23,883
He was incredibly sharp and always thoughtful.

201
00:12:23,906 --> 00:12:27,239
Nobunaga must have been pleased.

202
00:12:27,262 --> 00:12:28,795
It really does.

203
00:12:28,818 --> 00:12:33,952
Nobunaga adored him, and he rose through the ranks quickly.

204
00:12:33,975 --> 00:12:39,008
And eventually, he became the ruler of Japan.

205
00:12:39,031 --> 00:12:41,164
That’s right.

206
00:12:41,187 --> 00:12:46,830
No one else has gone from a poor farmer to a shogun—it’s unheard of.

207
00:12:46,854 --> 00:12:50,587
It’s truly a dream come true.

208
00:12:50,610 --> 00:12:52,410
Yes, he is.

209
00:12:52,433 --> 00:12:57,600
Hideyoshi loved extravagance.

210
00:12:57,623 --> 00:13:00,922
I heard he had a thing for gold.

211
00:13:00,945 --> 00:13:02,478
Yes, he is.

212
00:13:02,501 --> 00:13:08,268
He built golden rooms and even a golden castle.

213
00:13:08,291 --> 00:13:14,347
This Osaka Castle has beautiful gold decorations too.

214
00:13:14,370 --> 00:13:19,242
He also threw grand festivals to entertain people.

215
00:13:19,266 --> 00:13:23,139
He was such a vibrant and energetic leader.

216
00:13:23,163 --> 00:13:24,966
Yes, he is.

217
00:13:24,990 --> 00:13:29,661
That’s why he’s still so beloved in Osaka today.

218
00:13:29,684 --> 00:13:34,976
Learning about Hideyoshi’s life is so inspiring.

219
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:38,600
Let’s never give up and keep pushing forward!

220
00:13:38,623 --> 00:13:40,190
Yes, he is.

221
00:13:40,213 --> 00:13:45,646
Let’s go inside and check out the exhibits.

222
00:13:45,669 --> 00:13:47,169
Scene 6: Edo and Tokugawa Ieyasu

223
00:13:47,169 --> 00:13:51,702
Tokyo is such a lively city!

224
00:13:51,725 --> 00:13:53,497
It sure is.

225
00:13:53,521 --> 00:13:58,609
Tokyo was once called Edo.

226
00:13:58,632 --> 00:14:02,472
Who was the man behind Edo?

227
00:14:02,496 --> 00:14:06,963
The shogun Tokugawa Ieyasu.

228
00:14:06,986 --> 00:14:10,042
Tokugawa Ieyasu?

229
00:14:10,065 --> 00:14:13,556
What kind of person was he?

230
00:14:13,579 --> 00:14:19,027
He was incredibly patient—always biding his time.

231
00:14:19,051 --> 00:14:22,920
He was good at waiting?

232
00:14:22,943 --> 00:14:29,343
There’s a famous poem: 'If the cuckoo doesn’t sing, wait until it does.'

233
00:14:29,366 --> 00:14:31,733
What does that mean?

234
00:14:31,756 --> 00:14:38,033
It means waiting quietly for the birds to sing, even if they don't at first.

235
00:14:38,057 --> 00:14:40,066
I see.

236
00:14:40,089 --> 00:14:43,107
So, it's about not forcing things.

237
00:14:43,131 --> 00:14:44,768
Yes, he is.

238
00:14:44,792 --> 00:14:50,792
After the deaths of Nobunaga and Hideyoshi, he ultimately rose to the top.

239
00:14:50,815 --> 00:14:54,615
Did you struggle a lot when you were young?

240
00:14:54,638 --> 00:14:56,238
Yes, he is.

241
00:14:56,261 --> 00:15:01,531
As a child, I was held hostage and had no freedom.

242
00:15:01,554 --> 00:15:05,411
You endured a lot for a long time, didn't you?

243
00:15:05,435 --> 00:15:07,047
It really does.

244
00:15:07,070 --> 00:15:11,603
That’s why he understood the importance of peace.

245
00:15:11,626 --> 00:15:16,839
How long did the era that Ieyasu created last?

246
00:15:16,863 --> 00:15:21,758
Peace lasted for about 250 years.

247
00:15:21,781 --> 00:15:24,468
A full 250 years?

248
00:15:24,492 --> 00:15:27,786
That’s incredible.

249
00:15:27,809 --> 00:15:32,661
During that time, Japan saw no major wars.

250
00:15:32,685 --> 00:15:38,485
That’s why fun cultural traditions like rakugo and kabuki flourished.

251
00:15:38,508 --> 00:15:40,508
That’s right.

252
00:15:40,531 --> 00:15:45,098
Because of the peace, people could enjoy life.

253
00:15:45,120 --> 00:15:48,534
Did Ieyasu live a long life?

254
00:15:48,558 --> 00:15:54,240
For his time, he lived unusually long—until the age of 73.

255
00:15:54,263 --> 00:15:57,797
He must have taken good care of his health.

256
00:15:57,820 --> 00:15:59,420
Yes, he is.

257
00:15:59,443 --> 00:16:05,376
He was such a health enthusiast that he even made his own medicine.

258
00:16:05,399 --> 00:16:10,332
Success requires both health and patience, doesn’t it?

259
00:16:10,355 --> 00:16:15,370
There’s so much we can learn from Ieyasu’s way of life.

260
00:16:15,394 --> 00:16:20,528
Walking around the Imperial Palace, you can really feel the history.

261
00:16:20,551 --> 00:16:25,799
Yes, let’s take a leisurely stroll and enjoy it.

262
00:16:25,822 --> 00:16:27,322
Scene 7: The Charm of Sakamoto Ryoma

263
00:16:27,322 --> 00:16:33,122
This man in the photo looks so cool.

264
00:16:33,146 --> 00:16:37,279
That’s Sakamoto Ryoma.

265
00:16:37,302 --> 00:16:40,579
He’s a hero from Kochi Prefecture.

266
00:16:40,602 --> 00:16:43,569
Sakamoto Ryoma?

267
00:16:43,592 --> 00:16:48,959
Looking at his feet, he’s wearing shoes.

268
00:16:48,982 --> 00:16:52,230
You noticed that!

269
00:16:52,253 --> 00:16:57,886
He was the first samurai to wear boots.

270
00:16:57,909 --> 00:17:02,577
Not sandals, but leather boots?

271
00:17:02,601 --> 00:17:04,234
Yes, he is.

272
00:17:04,257 --> 00:17:08,835
He loved new foreign cultures.

273
00:17:08,859 --> 00:17:11,792
What did he do?

274
00:17:11,815 --> 00:17:18,548
He traveled all over Japan to build a new nation.

275
00:17:18,571 --> 00:17:21,625
Was he fighting?

276
00:17:21,648 --> 00:17:23,820
No.

277
00:17:23,844 --> 00:17:28,119
He was great at bringing people together through dialogue.

278
00:17:28,142 --> 00:17:33,175
He got rival groups to make peace?

279
00:17:33,198 --> 00:17:35,031
Yes, he is.

280
00:17:35,054 --> 00:17:40,888
He reconciled powerful factions like Satsuma and Choshu.

281
00:17:40,911 --> 00:17:44,244
That’s amazing.

282
00:17:44,267 --> 00:17:47,322
How did he persuade them?

283
00:17:47,345 --> 00:17:53,878
He told them, 'Let’s work together for the sake of Japan.'

284
00:17:53,901 --> 00:17:56,701
He had a broad perspective, didn’t he?

285
00:17:56,724 --> 00:17:58,535
It really does.

286
00:17:58,559 --> 00:18:03,226
Ryoma even founded a shipping company.

287
00:18:03,249 --> 00:18:06,459
Was that Japan’s first company?

288
00:18:06,482 --> 00:18:08,132
Yes, he is.

289
00:18:08,156 --> 00:18:12,823
He aimed to connect Japan with the world through trade.

290
00:18:12,846 --> 00:18:15,702
He had big dreams.

291
00:18:15,725 --> 00:18:21,492
He’s famous for saying he wanted to 'wash Japan.'

292
00:18:21,515 --> 00:18:24,374
Wash Japan?

293
00:18:24,398 --> 00:18:27,364
That’s an interesting way to put it.

294
00:18:27,387 --> 00:18:33,554
It means he wanted to cleanse and renew the old Japan.

295
00:18:33,577 --> 00:18:39,055
Did Ryoma get to see the new era of Japan?

296
00:18:39,078 --> 00:18:46,083
Unfortunately, he was assassinated at the age of 31.

297
00:18:46,106 --> 00:18:47,273
It really does.

298
00:18:47,296 --> 00:18:50,829
He died that young?

299
00:18:50,852 --> 00:18:52,785
Yes, he is.

300
00:18:52,808 --> 00:18:59,241
But his ideas helped shape the new Japan.

301
00:18:59,264 --> 00:19:04,864
I'd love to visit the large bronze statue of him standing by the sea in Kochi.

302
00:19:04,887 --> 00:19:06,703
It really does.

303
00:19:06,727 --> 00:19:12,194
The statue of Ryoma gazing at the ocean is truly moving.

304
00:19:12,217 --> 00:19:17,310
Let's plan a trip for the next holiday.

305
00:19:17,333 --> 00:19:19,066
That sounds great!

306
00:19:19,089 --> 00:19:22,423
And let's enjoy some delicious katsuo (bonito) too!

307
00:19:22,445 --> 00:19:23,945
Scene 8: The Appeal of Ninjas

308
00:19:23,945 --> 00:19:28,379
I watched a ninja action movie over the weekend.

309
00:19:28,402 --> 00:19:30,688
Was it good?

310
00:19:30,712 --> 00:19:32,278
Yes, he is.

311
00:19:32,302 --> 00:19:37,868
The ninjas were amazing—climbing walls and disappearing like magic!

312
00:19:37,891 --> 00:19:41,863
Movie ninjas seem almost supernatural, don’t they?

313
00:19:41,887 --> 00:19:47,220
Who is the most famous ninja in Japanese history?

314
00:19:47,243 --> 00:19:52,443
Hattori Hanzō is the most well-known.

315
00:19:52,466 --> 00:19:55,320
Hattori Hanzō?

316
00:19:55,344 --> 00:19:58,411
His name even appears in video games.

317
00:19:58,434 --> 00:20:00,168
It sure is.

318
00:20:00,191 --> 00:20:04,929
Hanzō was a ninja who protected Tokugawa Ieyasu.

319
00:20:04,952 --> 00:20:09,719
Did he wear black clothes and hide his face?

320
00:20:09,742 --> 00:20:14,601
Actually, he usually wore regular samurai attire.

321
00:20:14,624 --> 00:20:18,716
Oh, so he didn’t wear black?

322
00:20:18,740 --> 00:20:20,540
Yes, he is.

323
00:20:20,563 --> 00:20:25,396
Black would have made him stand out too much.

324
00:20:25,419 --> 00:20:27,687
That makes sense.

325
00:20:27,711 --> 00:20:31,444
What was a ninja’s real job?

326
00:20:31,467 --> 00:20:37,801
Gathering enemy intelligence and protecting important people.

327
00:20:37,824 --> 00:20:41,190
So it wasn’t just about fighting?

328
00:20:41,213 --> 00:20:46,624
Their main job was secretly transporting information without fighting.

329
00:20:46,648 --> 00:20:51,143
What did Hattori Hanzō accomplish?

330
00:20:51,166 --> 00:20:57,000
He helped Ieyasu escape safely over the mountains during a crisis.

331
00:20:57,023 --> 00:21:00,756
He saved Ieyasu’s life?

332
00:21:00,779 --> 00:21:02,412
Yes, he is.

333
00:21:02,435 --> 00:21:07,490
Thanks to Hanzō, Ieyasu survived.

334
00:21:07,513 --> 00:21:10,690
That’s an incredible achievement.

335
00:21:10,714 --> 00:21:16,994
His name lives on in Tokyo as Hanzōmon.

336
00:21:17,017 --> 00:21:21,665
Yes, it’s even a subway station name!

337
00:21:21,689 --> 00:21:23,822
That’s right.

338
00:21:23,845 --> 00:21:28,679
That’s where Hanzō’s house used to be.

339
00:21:28,702 --> 00:21:33,749
A ninja’s name became a station name?

340
00:21:33,772 --> 00:21:35,261
Yes, he is.

341
00:21:35,284 --> 00:21:39,484
He guarded the gates of Ieyasu’s castle.

342
00:21:39,507 --> 00:21:43,362
Ninjas feel so close to us now.

343
00:21:43,386 --> 00:21:47,998
The real history of ninjas is fascinating to learn about.

344
00:21:48,022 --> 00:21:52,855
I’ll read more books about ninjas.

345
00:21:52,878 --> 00:21:54,378
Scene 9: Yasuke: The Black Samurai of Japan

346
00:21:54,378 --> 00:22:00,811
I came across shocking news on the internet the other day.

347
00:22:00,834 --> 00:22:03,434
What kind of news?

348
00:22:03,457 --> 00:22:09,540
There was once a Black samurai in Japan.

349
00:22:09,564 --> 00:22:13,701
Ah, you mean Yasuke.

350
00:22:13,724 --> 00:22:17,291
Yes, Yasuke.

351
00:22:17,314 --> 00:22:20,847
Did he really exist?

352
00:22:20,870 --> 00:22:23,642
He did indeed.

353
00:22:23,666 --> 00:22:26,466
He was a samurai under Oda Nobunaga.

354
00:22:26,489 --> 00:22:32,056
Where did Yasuke come from before arriving in Japan?

355
00:22:32,079 --> 00:22:37,612
I came by ship from Africa with Italians.

356
00:22:37,635 --> 00:22:42,609
What did Nobunaga think when he first saw Yasuke?

357
00:22:42,632 --> 00:22:49,399
At first, he thought Yasuke's dark skin was painted with ink.

358
00:22:49,422 --> 00:22:52,655
Did he make Yasuke wash his body?

359
00:22:52,678 --> 00:22:59,612
Yes, and he was astonished when Yasuke's skin remained dark even after washing.

360
00:22:59,635 --> 00:23:04,929
So, Nobunaga took a liking to Yasuke, didn't he?

361
00:23:04,952 --> 00:23:12,634
That's right—Yasuke was incredibly tall and strong.

362
00:23:12,657 --> 00:23:17,457
It was said he had the strength of about ten men.

363
00:23:17,480 --> 00:23:24,556
Yes, Nobunaga gave him the name 'Yasuke.'

364
00:23:24,580 --> 00:23:27,858
And then he made him a samurai?

365
00:23:27,881 --> 00:23:34,718
Yes, he gave Yasuke a sword and a house, keeping him close by.

366
00:23:34,742 --> 00:23:38,949
Could Yasuke speak Japanese?

367
00:23:38,972 --> 00:23:44,788
He gradually learned Japanese and conversed with Nobunaga.

368
00:23:44,812 --> 00:23:49,453
Did he go into battle with Nobunaga?

369
00:23:49,476 --> 00:23:56,209
Yes, he fought by Nobunaga's side to protect him until the very end.

370
00:23:56,232 --> 00:24:01,917
It's incredible that a foreigner became a samurai.

371
00:24:01,941 --> 00:24:08,015
He's even a hot topic in overseas anime and movies now.

372
00:24:08,038 --> 00:24:11,646
People all over the world are paying attention, aren't they?

373
00:24:11,669 --> 00:24:18,403
Yes, it's a remarkable story of someone who thrived regardless of nationality.

374
00:24:18,426 --> 00:24:22,159
Now I really want to watch the anime about Yasuke.

375
00:24:22,182 --> 00:24:26,715
You should definitely check it out—it's really cool!

376
00:24:26,738 --> 00:24:28,238
Scene 10: Abe no Seimei’s Magic / Abe no Seimei: The Legendary Onmyoji

377
00:24:28,238 --> 00:24:34,972
I want to visit Seimei Shrine in Kyoto. Seimei Shrine?

378
00:24:34,995 --> 00:24:38,280
The shrine dedicated to Abe no Seimei, right?

379
00:24:38,303 --> 00:24:43,270
What kind of person was Abe no Seimei?

380
00:24:43,293 --> 00:24:49,660
He was someone who did work like a magician in ancient Japan.

381
00:24:49,683 --> 00:24:52,483
A magician?

382
00:24:52,506 --> 00:24:54,672
That sounds fascinating!

383
00:24:54,695 --> 00:24:58,065
He was called an Onmyoji.

384
00:24:58,089 --> 00:25:01,376
What kind of work did he do?

385
00:25:01,400 --> 00:25:07,253
He observed the stars and sky to predict the weather.

386
00:25:07,276 --> 00:25:09,916
He predicted the weather?

387
00:25:09,939 --> 00:25:11,511
Yes, he is.

388
00:25:11,534 --> 00:25:16,801
He also created calendars and divined fortunes.

389
00:25:16,824 --> 00:25:21,591
Is it true he exorcised evil spirits?

390
00:25:21,614 --> 00:25:29,080
People in the past believed Seimei could ward off illnesses and evil spirits.

391
00:25:29,103 --> 00:25:34,721
I heard he used invisible servants called Shikigami.

392
00:25:34,745 --> 00:25:36,721
That’s right.

393
00:25:36,745 --> 00:25:41,945
It's said he animated paper figures to do his bidding.

394
00:25:41,968 --> 00:25:46,101
It's like something straight out of a fantasy movie!

395
00:25:46,124 --> 00:25:47,730
Yes, he is.

396
00:25:47,754 --> 00:25:53,687
That's why he's often the main character in novels, movies, and manga.

397
00:25:53,710 --> 00:25:59,684
Figure skater Yuzuru Hanyu used a piece inspired by Seimei, didn't he?

398
00:25:59,707 --> 00:26:01,474
Yes, he is.

399
00:26:01,497 --> 00:26:05,764
He won a gold medal with that program.

400
00:26:05,787 --> 00:26:10,157
His costume and performance were so mystical.

401
00:26:10,180 --> 00:26:15,788
The poses and pentagram symbol are Seimei's trademarks.

402
00:26:15,811 --> 00:26:18,778
The five-pointed star symbol, right?

403
00:26:18,801 --> 00:26:20,434
Yes, he is.

404
00:26:20,457 --> 00:26:25,304
You'll find that star symbol all over the shrine.

405
00:26:25,328 --> 00:26:28,225
Does it have a meaning for warding off evil?

406
00:26:28,249 --> 00:26:30,315
That’s right.

407
00:26:30,338 --> 00:26:33,872
It's a symbol that drives away evil.

408
00:26:33,895 --> 00:26:37,579
How long did Seimei live?

409
00:26:37,602 --> 00:26:42,808
He lived to be over 80 and helped many people.

410
00:26:42,831 --> 00:26:48,565
Living to 80 in those times was incredibly long-lived!

411
00:26:48,589 --> 00:26:50,047
It really does.

412
00:26:50,071 --> 00:26:55,042
Perhaps they possessed an extraordinary gift—maybe they were a genius.

413
00:26:55,065 --> 00:26:59,509
I want to buy a good luck charm at the shrine to boost my fortune.

414
00:26:59,533 --> 00:27:02,902
Let’s go together and pay our respects.